\begin_layout Title

\lang english
Appendix:
 Age and Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
Now that we have found the state-dependent evolutionarily stable strategy for a given set of parameter values,
 along with the associated demographically stable distribution of individual physiological states and patch density states,
 we can ask what the ESS behaviour of a cohort of individuals within such a population would look like.
 We can assume that one small cohort following the same ESS will not have an influence on the distribution of states of the larger population and so we do not need to update any of the transition probabilities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang english
Birth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
We know that they will begin life at the minimum size and quality states and with their belief state being the evolved prior for the stable patch distribution prior to dispersal,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches prior to dispersal that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{0}=1-\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision process for we need interpolate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete probability values,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=i\in\{0/I,1/I,2/I,...,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is an integer determining the fineness of the grid.
 The first step of interpolation is to find the floor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

when it is scaled by the grid size,
 which will give us the nearest grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 below the continuous value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\eta_{1}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the difference is the fractional part 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\eta_{1}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset

We can then use the fractional part as a probability that the state should be interpolated to be 
\begin_inset Formula $(i+1)$
\end_inset

,
 finally giving us two discrete states and associated probabilities for defining the state distribution of this cohort as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i,t}(s,\pi=i,m)=1-f
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i,t}(s,\pi=i+1,m)=f
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

